Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
so sam tell us more  good morning adam with 51 days to go  until britain hosts the global climate  change summit cop in glasgow we can  reveal that ministers have bowed to  pressure from australia  to scrub two very specific commitments  from the uk australia trade deal which  is still in negotiation now you might  have thought that that was done and  dusted three months ago when boris  johnson met his australian counterpart  scott morrison but we are told that  actually some of the most difficult asks  are happening right now and it's not yet  concluded even though britain hopes to  get it over the line within weeks i've  obtained an email a leaked email from  the cabinet office a senior official  that revealed what went on last month  this said that after discussions  involving lord frost and uh  and liz trust liz trust and quasiquating  met the note says the business secretary  has agreed that in order to get the  australia fta free trade agreement over  the line dit the department for trade  can drop both of the climate asks i.e on  precedence of multilateral environment  agreements over fta provisions and a  reference to the paris agreement  temperature goals now you remember that  there was a previous cop summit in paris  that successfully uh bound the various  nations to reducing their uh emissions  by specific amounts and whilst i  understand that the uk australia trade  deal will contain references broader  references to the paris agreement it's  those temperature  commitments that will be dropped now  raises two questions one how usual is it  in trade agreements to have reference to  climate change and two why would the  australians want this dropped well  that's a very good question because uh  it was deemed important enough for  specific climate change temperature  commitments to be included in the uk eu  deal  also up until about a month ago britain  was expecting that the temperature  commitments would go in the australia  deal as well now clearly australia have  made this ask and uk ministers have  agreed to this ask for a reason  australia defends its climate change  record but many environmentalists point  out that it's quite dependent on coal  that some of its senior political  figures are less committed to climate  change than others now australia says it  will still meet its paris commitment and  has signed other agreements uh that  commit them to specific temperature  changes but nevertheless i think green  groups are extremely concerned uh  because as i say australia will have  asked this for a reason let's hear the  executive director of greenpeace john  savin  if we cannot get a rich country like  australia to take the environment  seriously to take the climate emergency  seriously then we are in really really  big trouble if the government is giving  in on these environmental regulations it  is truly shocking and it is truly  seismic in terms of the impact that that  will have chris johnson in a letter to  me and other environmental  leaders said that the trade agreement  will include commitments to all  multilateral environment agreements  including the paris agreement now the  reason why he wrote this letter was  because he was basically saying trade  and the environment cannot be separated  he said this is not going to be a race  to the bottom we're not going to  undercut regulations we're not going to  undercut the commitments that we've made  to deal with the climate emergency in  fact he said the very opposite there's  going to be a race for the top now what  this email shows is that that is just a  bare-faced lie that actually what  they're doing is the opposite this is a  race to the bottom and it is a disaster  so sam where does this leave boris  johnson and the government which  obviously want trade deals but they also  want breakthroughs on uh climate change  commitments well i think the government  are now scrambling to put a brave face  on this now they're going to claim uh  that they've secured a first when it  comes to the climate goals in the  australia trade deal they'll point out  that for the first time australia has  reference will reference the paris  agreement in a trade deal they haven't  done that  before  but the truth is that the binding  elements have clearly been dropped  and that'll happen  for a reason so  government sources to me talk about the  commitments now being implicit rather  than legally blinding and explicit i  suspect that opposition mps trade groups  and environmental groups  will make whatever they can of of that  very important uh change in language  there will nevertheless still be a whole  chapter on the environment in this deal  but it just doesn't contain some of the  things that environmental groups want to  see and increasingly australia looking  like one of the bad guys when it comes  to climate change well they say that  even though they have uh not committed  as much as some other countries like the  uk when it comes to uh when it comes to  climate change and uh  reducing their emissions there is a hope  that australia is one of the nations  that moves uh before the climate change  summit in early november uh and  increases its targets as interim targets  uh towards meeting the paris climate  change agreement we will see whether  they do that i think they would say that  you need to look at particularly the  behavior of china uh first before uh  putting pressure on countries like them  they do link the importance of the coal  industry in australia uh to jobs and  suggest that any attempt to get rid of  the coal industry or reduce it in size  would have an economic impact that they  are reluctant to hit but that debate  clearly very live in australia not not  these given recent climate change events  there like the fires

